00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.360 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it was past midnight but I was ready the  next day I meet with the CEO and other  senior leaders of my global consulting  firm I talk about the work we were doing  as employee resource groups and our  progress in diversity and inclusion for  the past two years I had been leading  our LGBTQ and Ally employee resource  group and we were doing great things  we had expanded to over 25 offices  globally we were increasing awareness  and support across geographies these  were just a few of the wins that I was  really excited to talk about the next  day earlier I had rehearsed with the  head of our diversity and inclusion team  she was thrilled by the presentation  there was just one last thing that I had  to do she asked me if I was going to  wear my nail polish to the meeting  now I didn't think much of it in the  moment I would often take off my nail  polish to meet with clients but that  night it felt different I couldn't help  but feel as I went finger by finger that  I was removing a part of myself now I  get that nail polish seems like  something trivial but to me it is so  tied to my identity it's like my cross  or my hijab and that I wear it as a  symbol to the world about my queerness  and I wear it as a symbol to myself  about Who I am and my journey I wear it  like armor to feel confident and to  overcome the insecurities that I have  felt for so many years so that night I  had an aha moment I thought maybe I've  slipped through a crack in our DNI  tonight I'm gonna talk about the work  being done in diversity and inclusion  I'm gonna talk about what's going well  and how it can be improved in doing so  I'm going to emphasize the importance of  thinking more about individuality in our  worked in DNI as practitioners business  leaders and people so for those of you  who aren't quite sure what DNI is  diversity and inclusion  it's a firm's formal set of activities  to create a workplace where people of  all backgrounds feel welcomed and valued  it includes things like non-biased  trainings mentorship programs and  recruitment initiatives when it's done  well it can have business impacts in  fact in a recent survey 85% of CEOs so  that DNI programs were improving their  bottom lines they talked about things  like greater innovation access to new  markets and retaining talent better so  it's clear that Dee and I can improve  business performance but DNI is not just  about bottom lines Wendy and I has done  well it can have big impacts on  individuals that means all of us even  with my story about the nail polish I'd  still argue that DNI is essential as a  queer person I have a long complicated  relationship with my identity so when  I'm in a workplace where I feel included  I don't have to stress about fitting in  or if I'm being judged I can put all of  my brainpower into my work I deliver  better results and I feel motivated to  do so now I assume others in the  audience tonight have had similar  experiences about DNI and feeling  included and that underscores the value  of DNI for all of us so now that we've  talked a little bit about why DNI is  important I want us to think about the  ways that our current approaches may  actually be holding us back  so traditionally DNI is organized around  categorical groups think women veterans  people of color this is great and it  does bring a lot of value to firms  but it's not perfect  I'll focus on two limitations in  particular the first is how we think  about the diversity within categories  and the second is how we handle  intersectionality which is the interplay  across our different identities so for  this first idea of the diversity within  categories let's go back to my original  story it's true that we were having a  lot of success for the LGBTQ community  but as I realized firsthand  it wasn't success for everyone in that  community  more specifically we weren't doing  enough for trans and queer people like  myself see there was a problem with  working with a group we weren't seeing  who wasn't at the table and we were  getting a false positive on our success  so as companies work on D&amp;I they need to  look critically at the converse at the  composition of their diverse communities  ask in my company does LGBTQ actually  include l.g be T and Q in any company is  every woman white or is every black  employee a man we need to take these  into consideration to advance our DNI so  having talked about this first  limitation around the diversity within  categories let's think about the second  limitation around intersectionality  again this is the interplay across our  different identities and for this I'm  going to talk about the experience of my  friend Erika a black woman again  traditional DNI would organize programs  around categorical groups and there  would be programs for black employees  and others for women and Erika would opt  in to both but in her words she wouldn't  really be there as her full self she'd  be black Eric out of lunch and later  that day  woman Erika at a dinner it sounds  strange right now we're all different  but we're all similar and that we are  all complex  we're all a combination of identities  some visible most invisible and so we  can all have this experience where it's  hard to be present as our full selves  when we're only being viewed as  categories so when working in D&amp;I we  need to make sure we're designing it for  whole people so having talked about  these two limitations the first the  diversity within categories and the  second intersectionality let's think of  what we can do to get beyond them I  would suggest that working more around  this idea of individuality is a start  and what might that look like well could  take the shape of storytelling think of  the impact of erica's and my stories  tonight think of the impact they and  others could have in sparking  discussions and shaping workplace  culture it could take the shape of  events that celebrate intersectionality  and all of our identities visible and  invisible taken together these kinds of  programs help us overcome the current  limitations of our work we can see and  support new pools of talent we can let  people show up and participate as their  whole selves and not just their parts  each of these incremental gains improves  inclusivity which should attract new  forms of diversity which should lead to  even better outcomes when I reflect on  my experience I probably wouldn't have  joined my firm if it didn't have DNI I  probably would have opted out of  consulting and business altogether for  me it would have been easier not to  exist in those worlds than to feel  unwelcome or excluded but what if I did  what value would have been lost to my  company my clients and myself  DNI gave me a reason to opt in and  it's not perfect it's certainly part of  the reason why I'm here now so I'm  preparing for this moment I had to ask  myself should I take off my nail polish  I can still be insecure in a lot of  settings especially ones like this but  I'm proud to stand up here tonight armor  on and ready to go to battle for a  greater focus on individuality in our  work in DNI because diversity and  inclusion is business  smart but designing it for individuals  that's what makes it human thank you  [Music]

